// Appending to a string literal through the buffer primitives.
//
// `str_buf_append` takes a buffer and returns the buffer with the text appended.
// The buffer it is handed does not have to be one it allocated: a literal is a
// valid argument, and it has a header with a capacity of zero because there is
// nothing to release.
//
// The growth test is `capacity - length < wanted`, which is `0 - 0 < 0`, and
// that is false. So appending nothing to nothing reallocated nothing, and the
// terminator was then written through the literal's own bytes -- which are in
// `.rodata`, because the program cannot change them. The store is the crash, and
// it is a store rather than a load, so the address is a good one and the program
// dies with no output at all.
//
// This is a one-line case on purpose. The whole of it is "the buffer was not one
// the runtime owns, and appending nothing to it must still produce a buffer that
// is".

int main() {
    // Nothing appended to a literal: the case that crashed.
    Console.WriteLog(len(str_buf_append("", "")));

    // Something appended to a literal, which reallocates and so never crashed.
    Console.WriteLog(str_buf_append("", "tail"));

    // A literal of non-zero length as the buffer. Its capacity is still zero, and
    // its length is not, so the subtraction is negative rather than zero.
    Console.WriteLog(str_buf_append("abc", ""));

    // And the ordinary case, a buffer the runtime did allocate, unchanged.
    var b = str_buf_new(0);
    Console.WriteLog(len(str_buf_append(b, "grown")));
    return 0;
}
